"A government that can stop a thief can also stop a reader, a drinker, or a preacher. Every society therefore has to decide which of the things people do it will leave alone. The decisions are familiar ones. May the law require a motorcyclist to wear a helmet? May it forbid an adult to take a drug that harms only himself, or a newspaper to print what most people find disgusting? For most of history the answer was that rulers may forbid whatever is bad for the people or offensive to the gods. In 1859 John Stuart Mill argued, in a short book called On Liberty, that this answer is wrong.": "cc2e11a6fe20",
"China has an older tradition of governing by leaving alone. The Daodejing says that the more prohibitions there are, the poorer the people become, and that the more laws are published, the more thieves there are. Its sage says: I do nothing, and the people transform themselves. Yet Laozi is no Mill. The same book advises the ruler to empty the peoples minds and fill their bellies, and praises the ancients who kept the people simple and did not enlighten them. Laozi wanted a government that did not meddle and a people that did not argue. Mill wanted the argument above all. The Legalist Han Fei drew the opposite conclusion from both: in the state of an enlightened ruler there are no books, and the law is the teaching. The disagreement among the three is one that every government still has to settle.": "aa62d1efcbd4",
"Critics have pressed two points.": "09a98d96f9e4",
"Critics have pressed two points. The first is that almost nothing harms only oneself. The drinker has a family and the injured rider needs a hospital bed. Mill answered that we should punish the neglect of a definite duty, such as failing to support ones children, and leave the drinking alone. The second is that the word harm is asked to do too much work. Should deep offense count as harm? Mill said no for opinions, but he accepted laws against public indecency. In the 1950s the English judge Patrick Devlin argued that a society may defend its shared morality as it defends itself against treason. The legal philosopher H. L. A. Hart replied, in Mills spirit, that disgust is no proof of harm.": "630cef4583a7",
"Every society has to decide what it will leave alone.": "c1e77c40b4b3",
"In 1958 Isaiah Berlin pointed out that Mills question is only one of two that go by the name of freedom. Negative liberty is the absence of interference: how many doors are open to me. Positive liberty is self-mastery: who is in control. An addict whom no one stops has the first and lacks the second. Berlin thought the positive idea the more dangerous in politics. Once a ruler claims to know what my true self wants, he can coerce me and call it liberation, and Rousseau had spoken of forcing a man to be free. Others replied that negative liberty alone is a thin thing to someone too poor or too ignorant to walk through any of the open doors.": "e6bad5dc5721",
"Laozi wanted a government that did not meddle.": "6967b22ca83c",
"Laws that cannot answer the question have a bad record.": "24f590eb6c73",
"Mill proposed what he called one very simple principle. It reads: the only purpose for which power can be rightfully exercised over any member of a civilised community, against his will, is to prevent harm to others. His own good, either physical or moral, is not a sufficient warrant. We may argue with a man who is ruining his health, plead with him, or avoid his company. We may not force him. Over himself, over his own body and mind, the individual is sovereign.": "fa072b0f1bbb",
"Mill was a utilitarian, and he does not appeal to natural rights. He argues that liberty makes human life go better. The case is clearest for speech. If we silence an opinion, it may be true, and we lose the chance of exchanging error for truth. No one who silences is infallible. If the opinion is false, we still lose, because a truth that is never challenged comes to be held as a dead dogma, not a living truth. People repeat it without knowing why it is so. He who knows only his own side of the case, knows little of that. And most opinions in dispute are partly true, so that each side needs the other to correct it. Hence his most quoted sentence: If all mankind minus one, were of one opinion, and only one person were of the contrary opinion, mankind would be no more justified in silencing that one person, than he, if he had the power, would be justified in silencing mankind.": "d9f1b8f50ebb",
"On Berlins second question the Buddha goes much further than Mill. Mill calls a person free when no one stops him from doing what he desires. The Buddha asks whether the desire is free. One discourse puts it this way: The world is wanting, insatiable, the slave of craving. A person driven by craving, anger, and confusion has no outside master and is still unfree. The word for the goal of the path, vimutti, means release. This is positive liberty in its strongest form, and it avoids the danger Berlin saw in the idea. No one can be forced into it, since the release is something a person has to see for himself. A verse of the Dhammapada says, One is indeed the lord of oneself, for who else would be ones lord? (Dharma and Western Philosophy D18).": "00ff899e6e89",
"On Mills question the Buddha is close to Mill. He founded no state and gave laypeople no laws. The five precepts are undertaken by choice and are not enforced by anyone. He told the Kālāmas not to go by tradition or scripture or by the thought The ascetic is our respected teacher, and to accept a teaching only when you know for yourselves what it leads to. He held that no one can be made good from outside: You yourselves must do the work, the Realized Ones just show the way. And again: Purity and impurity are personal matters, no one can purify another. A Buddhist argument for the harm principle would rest on this last point. Virtue that is forced on a person has no value as virtue.": "7927cc0bf8eb",
"Such laws are defended on the ground that hate speech harms its targets.": "2061b823452d",
"That principle is, that the sole end for which mankind are warranted, individually or collectively, in interfering with the liberty of action of any of their number, is self-protection. That the only purpose for which power can be rightfully exercised over any member of a civilised community, against his will, is to prevent harm to others. His own good, either physical or moral, is not a sufficient warrant. He cannot rightfully be compelled to do or forbear because it will be better for him to do so, because it will make him happier, because, in the opinions of others, to do so would be wise, or even right. [] In the part which merely concerns himself, his independence is, of right, absolute. Over himself, over his own body and mind, the individual is sovereign.": "70fa4d3b9784",
"The more prohibitions there are in the world, the poorer the people become. The more sharp tools the people have, the more disordered the state. The more skills men acquire, the more strange things appear. The more laws and orders are made known, the more thieves and robbers there are. Therefore the sage says: I do nothing, and the people transform themselves. I love stillness, and the people set themselves right. I undertake nothing, and the people grow rich of themselves. I have no desires, and the people return of themselves to simplicity.": "4e704b2782ad",
"The principle is simple to state and hard to apply, and Mill knew it. He distinguished an opinion from an incitement. The view that corn dealers starve the poor may be printed freely, but it may be punished when delivered orally to an excited mob assembled before the house of a corn-dealer. He allowed one case of protecting a person from himself. A man who sees another about to step onto an unsafe bridge may seize him, since he does not desire to fall into the river. And he held that the law should refuse to enforce a contract by which a person sells himself into slavery: The principle of freedom cannot require that he should be free not to be free.": "48df565405a3",
"The same reasoning covers ways of living. No one knows in advance what kind of life is best for human beings, so there should be different experiments of living. A person who lets custom choose for him needs no faculty, Mill says, but the ape-like one of imitation. Choosing for oneself is how judgment and character are exercised, and a people that is not allowed to choose will stop improving.": "3eb2ff76c226",
"This is hard to square with what he says about offensive opinions.": "1abc1311ea23",
"Two features of the principle are easy to miss. It applies to social pressure as well as to law. Mill feared what he called the tyranny of the majority, by which he meant the power of neighbors and employers to make an unusual persons life impossible, and he thought it reached further than any police. And the principle gives a necessary condition for interference. Harm to others makes interference permissible, but whether to interfere remains a further question. Competition harms the losing shopkeeper, and no one proposes to forbid it.": "33f22f1e7dfb",
"Two questions go by the name of freedom.": "128ecfef0623",
"We may plead with him. We may not force him.": "24cfff4fb5f9",
"Which parts of a life are nobodys business but ones own?": "934a4c3d1d55",
